WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.343
Real-time applications need operational
visibility,

2
00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:11.520
but visibility should not become data
exposure.

3
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.946
Useful SignalR logs include connection
attempts,

4
00:00:17.026 --> 00:00:24.610
authentication failures, authorization
denials, rejected messages,

5
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:31.901
validation failures, disconnect reasons,
unexpected exceptions,

6
00:00:31.981 --> 00:00:37.575
group membership changes, administrative
actions,

7
00:00:37.655 --> 00:00:41.758
and unusual resource-control events.

8
00:00:41.838 --> 00:00:45.941
Sensitive data should stay out of logs.

9
00:00:46.021 --> 00:00:52.734
Do not log passwords, tokens, secrets, raw
sensitive payloads,

10
00:00:52.814 --> 00:00:58.409
or full query strings that may contain
access tokens.

11
00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:06.694
Logging should also avoid excessive
personal data and should respect the same

12
00:01:06.774 --> 00:01:14.358
retention, access control, and review
standards used for the rest of the

13
00:01:14.438 --> 00:01:15.930
application.

14
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.491
Error behavior deserves review.

15
00:01:19.571 --> 00:01:25.414
SignalR returns generic error information
by default,

16
00:01:25.494 --> 00:01:30.467
which is usually safer for production
clients.

17
00:01:30.547 --> 00:01:33.904
Custom hub errors can be useful,

18
00:01:33.984 --> 00:01:42.189
but they should be written for the
receiving audience and checked for internal

19
00:01:42.269 --> 00:01:49.604
details, identifiers, stack information,
sensitive business data,

20
00:01:49.684 --> 00:01:53.290
and confusing recovery guidance.

21
00:01:53.370 --> 00:02:01.326
Development error detail should not
silently become production behavior.

22
00:02:01.406 --> 00:02:07.871
Deployment review should bring the earlier
topics together.

23
00:02:07.951 --> 00:02:15.535
Confirm HTTPS, CORS, WebSocket origin
behavior, access token logging,

24
00:02:15.615 --> 00:02:21.458
reverse proxies, load balancers, scale-out
settings,

25
00:02:21.538 --> 00:02:29.246
Azure SignalR Service or backplane
configuration, secrets management,

26
00:02:29.326 --> 00:02:35.915
dependency review, monitoring, alerting,
incident handoff,

27
00:02:35.995 --> 00:02:38.979
and documented exceptions.

28
00:02:39.059 --> 00:02:45.773
A good operational baseline makes security
review repeatable.

29
00:02:45.853 --> 00:02:52.442
Teams know which events are logged, which
values are redacted,

30
00:02:52.522 --> 00:02:58.489
which deployment settings matter, who
receives alerts,

31
00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:02.050
and how exceptions are approved.

32
00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:10.957
That practical discipline is what turns
SignalR security from a one-time checklist

33
00:03:11.037 --> 00:03:15.140
into an ongoing engineering practice.
